# Context Menu System Implementation Checklist

Status: planned

## Goal

Add a reusable DwarfUI context-menu system whose registration and target
resolution resemble the existing tooltip system while providing synchronous
right-click ownership and an interactive menu.

A caller registers a small `ContextMenuDefinition` against either a UI widget
or an exact map tile. Right-clicking an eligible registered target opens one
menu and consumes the actionable right-click input batch. UI-widget menus are
anchored to the captured screen position. Map-tile menus retain their world
position and are reprojected before every render so they remain attached while
the map viewport moves.

## Locked product decisions

- [ ] All context-menu-owned production modules live under
      `src/scripts_modinstalled/dwarfui/context_menu/`.
- [ ] All focused unit and DwarfSpec files live under
      `tests/context_menu/`.
- [ ] The public facade is loaded with
      `reqscript('dwarfui/context_menu/api')`; do not add a top-level
      `dwarfui/context_menu.lua` compatibility facade.
- [ ] A UI-widget registration opens at the screen position captured from the
      opening right-click.
- [ ] A map-tile registration opens from an exact world tile and recomputes
      its projected screen position before every render.
- [ ] "Screen position" means the DFHack screen/interface coordinate space
      used by widget frames, mouse input, and interface painting. It does not
      mean an operating-system physical display pixel.
- [ ] Screen-position anchors remain fixed for the open session, apart from
      menu-frame reclamping after an interface-size change.
- [ ] The preferred menu frame starts with its top-left cell at the captured
      or projected anchor. If that frame overflows, place the menu to the left
      and/or above the anchor before applying final screen-edge clamping.
- [ ] Map placement uses the canonical interface cell returned by the shared
      world-to-interface projection for the exact registered tile. It does not
      invent a separate tile-center or physical-pixel coordinate.
- [ ] Map-tile anchors move with camera panning and zoom and close when the
      source tile is no longer projectable on the displayed z-level or inside
      the visible map viewport.
- [ ] Opening occurs on the actionable `_MOUSE_R` event. When that event
      resolves a registered target, the complete input batch containing it is
      consumed so native DF and other UI cannot also react.
- [ ] The initial design has no mouse-down latch or post-dismissal trailing
      boundary suppression. `_MOUSE_R_DOWN` and `_MOUSE_L_DOWN` alone are not
      opening, dismissal, or selection events. Phase 0 must verify this against
      the active DFHack input seams before implementation proceeds.
- [ ] `LEAVESCREEN`/Escape closes the open menu and consumes the input.
- [ ] A second actionable `_MOUSE_R` closes the open menu, consumes its complete
      input batch, and does not reopen another target.
- [ ] Left-clicking an entry closes the menu, consumes the input, and invokes
      that entry exactly once.
- [ ] Left-clicking outside the menu on actionable `_MOUSE_L` closes it and
      consumes the complete input batch.
- [ ] Selecting an entry by keyboard also closes before invoking its handler
      and consumes the selection input.
- [ ] The definition used by an open menu is a validated snapshot. Updating a
      registration affects its next opening, not the current session.
- [ ] Snapshot isolation applies to definition table structure and field
      values. An `on_select` function retains ordinary Lua closure semantics
      for any external state captured by that function.
- [ ] Entry colors resolve independently in this order: entry override,
      menu-level default, system default.
- [ ] The initial system uses `COLOR_WHITE` as the system foreground default
      and `COLOR_BLACK` as the system background default.
- [ ] UI-widget targets take precedence over map-tile targets. A blocking
      DwarfUI widget region suppresses map fallback within the traversed
      DwarfUI root even when that region has no context menu.
- [ ] Within one DwarfUI root, reverse render order determines the hit result.
      Across multiple eligible DwarfUI roots, the registered widget with the
      latest original registration sequence wins, matching the tooltip target
      detector. Re-registering the same widget does not change that sequence.
- [ ] A registered widget target found in any eligible root wins over blockers
      in other roots. If no registered widget target is found, a blocker in any
      traversed eligible root suppresses map fallback.
- [ ] The menu closes before calling `on_select` so handler errors, reloads,
      or reentrant menu operations cannot leave stale presentation state.
- [ ] Context-menu presentation may own focus and input while open. It does
      not inherit the tooltip requirement to remain transparent to focus and
      input.
- [ ] Registration may install a reversible, generation-owned wrapper around
      an attached owning Lua screen instance's effective `onInput()` method.
      Hook ownership, restoration, reload invalidation, and foreign-wrapper
      behavior match `dwarfui/tooltip_render_hook.lua`: restore the predecessor
      exactly while DwarfUI remains outermost; if a foreign wrapper is
      outermost, preserve it and leave DwarfUI's superseded trampoline inert
      and diagnosable.
- [ ] Opening hooks only resolve and consume an actionable opening input. While
      a context-menu ZScreen is visible, that screen is the physical input
      surface for menu interaction and dismissal.
- [ ] An already-open menu is never replaced by another right-click. A second
      right-click only closes and consumes. Replacement is reserved for a
      future explicit programmatic API and is not part of the initial contract.
- [ ] Menu subviews get first refusal on keyboard input. Pause is an explicit
      forwarding exception for both anchor kinds. Only pause and unhandled
      map-movement keys may be forwarded to the parent of a map-tile menu; only
      pause may be forwarded for a screen-position menu.
- [ ] Input arrives as one key table. If a context-menu-owned key is present,
      consume the complete table. Otherwise forward only a filtered table
      containing an allowed pause or map-movement key and required modifiers.
      A genuine opening miss delegates the original table unchanged and
      preserves the predecessor's exact returns.
- [ ] No implementation or test may rely on loose discriminator strings or
      magic numbers. Closed sets use immutable numeric LuaDoc enums and named
      members.
- [ ] Refactor the shared `dwarfui/pointer` API to expose immutable numeric
      `PointerPolicy` and `PointerResultKind` enums, and migrate all existing
      production consumers and tests from string policies/results in the same
      change.
- [ ] Every new or modified class and method has LuaDoc documentation.
- [ ] Local unit evidence, packaged-module evidence, registered live-runtime
      evidence, and manual visual evidence are recorded separately.

## Intended public contract

```lua
local context_menu = reqscript('dwarfui/context_menu/api')

context_menu.register(widget, {
    title='Route stop',
    fg=COLOR_WHITE,
    bg=COLOR_BLACK,
    entries={
        {
            label='Move stop',
            on_select=function(context)
                -- Update the selected stop.
            end,
        },
        {
            label='Delete stop',
            fg=COLOR_LIGHTRED,
            on_select=function(context)
                -- Delete the selected stop.
            end,
        },
    },
})

local handle = context_menu.register_map_tile{
    owner=overlay,
    pos=stop.pos,
    definition=definition,
}
```

The implementation must expose `update(widget, definition)`,
`unregister(widget)`, atomic
`update_map_tile(handle, {pos=pos, definition=definition})`, and
`unregister_map_tile(handle)` operations. `register(widget, definition)` returns
whether it created a new registration; re-registration replaces the definition
and returns false. Update and unregister operations return false for an unknown
widget or handle and true after a successful mutation. Invalid definitions or
positions raise before changing stored state. Callers never mutate a registered
definition table by side effect.

## Planned module layout

```text
src/scripts_modinstalled/dwarfui/
  context_menu/
    api.lua
    definition.lua
    input_hook.lua
    input_sample.lua
    map_target.lua
    projection.lua
    registration.lua
    renderer.lua
    root_discovery.lua
    screen.lua
    service.lua
    target_detector.lua
    target.lua
```

Map projection may live outside this directory only when the same change makes
it a real shared dependency of at least two production consumers, including
migration of the existing minecart projection. Otherwise the projection
module remains context-menu-owned and lives under `context_menu/`. The
context-menu adapter, session logic, and focused tests always remain inside
their dedicated directories. The separately approved shared pointer enum
refactor modifies `dwarfui/pointer.lua` and its existing consumers; it is shared
infrastructure rather than a context-menu-owned module.

## Phase 0 - Freeze runtime seams and the API contract

- [ ] Record the active DwarfUI revision, branch, and worktree state before
      implementation.
- [ ] Record the active DFHack version and source revision used to audit input,
      `gui.ZScreen`, overlay, widget, and viewport behavior.
- [ ] Trace the current tooltip registration, root resolution, target
      detection, weak ownership, reload, and map-target behavior and identify
      the exact contracts to reuse.
- [ ] Confirm that tooltip pointer polling is not an opening-input seam because
      it runs after input and cannot consume a right-click.
- [ ] Verify the native input contract from
      `plugins.overlay.feed_viewscreen_widgets()` through the C++ overlay feed
      hook, including the meaning of a truthy Lua return.
- [ ] Verify the effective `onInput()` seam for Lua `gui.Screen` and
      `gui.ZScreen` instances, including predecessor call and return behavior.
- [ ] Record which right-click keys the active DFHack build can deliver,
      including `_MOUSE_R_DOWN` and `_MOUSE_R`, and whether they can be
      delivered separately or together in one key table.
- [ ] Verify from active DFHack source and a focused seam characterization that
      `_MOUSE_R_DOWN` alone does not perform the native or DwarfUI action that
      the context menu must suppress. If this is false, amend this checklist
      before implementing a latch or suppression mechanism.
- [ ] Record which left-click keys the active DFHack build and `widgets.List`
      consume, and verify that actionable selection and outside-click behavior
      occurs on `_MOUSE_L`, not `_MOUSE_L_DOWN`.
- [ ] Characterize mixed-key input tables and record which modifier keys must
      accompany a filtered pause or map-movement input sent to the parent.
- [ ] Verify `gui.ZScreen` parent render, focus, dismissal, pause, movement,
      and `sendInputToParent()` behavior.
- [ ] Verify native `widgets.List` keyboard, mouse, wheel, selection, and
      submission behavior and identify which row-painting behavior requires a
      context-menu-specific renderer.
- [ ] Audit the existing minecart world-to-interface projection in text and
      Premium graphics modes and decide whether to extract a generic helper or
      consume an existing public helper.
- [ ] Define immutable numeric `ContextMenuTargetKind` members for
      `WIDGET` and `MAP_TILE`.
- [ ] Define immutable numeric `ContextMenuAnchorKind` members for
      `SCREEN_POSITION` and `MAP_TILE`.
- [ ] Define immutable numeric `PointerPolicy` members for `TARGET`, `PASS`,
      `BLOCK`, and `NONE`, and `PointerResultKind` members for `TARGET`,
      `BLOCKED`, and `MISS` in the shared pointer module.
- [ ] Inventory every production and test consumer of string
      `pointer_policy`/pointer-result values so the shared enum migration is
      complete and atomic.
- [ ] Keep target identity and anchor placement as separate concepts throughout
      the API and internal session model.
- [ ] Do not publish an explicit programmatic `open_at_*` or replacement API
      in the initial contract. Reevaluate only when a concrete consumer and
      focused tests require one.

### Phase 0 completion gate

- [ ] The opening, closing, consumption, rendering, and projection seams are
      supported by the active DFHack source rather than assumptions.
- [ ] The public registration contract and both anchor modes are written down
      before production modules are added.
- [ ] Any deviation from the architecture in this checklist is recorded in
      this planning document before implementation proceeds.

## Phase 1 - Implement definitions, snapshots, and open-session types

- [ ] Add `context_menu/definition.lua` with documented
      `ContextMenuDefinition` and `ContextMenuEntry` annotations.
- [ ] Support an optional non-empty title after validation.
- [ ] Support menu-level optional `fg` and `bg` colors.
- [ ] Require a non-empty `entries` list.
- [ ] Require every entry to contain a non-empty string `label`.
- [ ] Require every entry to contain a callable `on_select`.
- [ ] Support independent optional entry-level `fg` and `bg` colors.
- [ ] Reject unknown shapes and invalid values with errors that identify the
      offending field and entry index.
- [ ] Resolve missing colors through entry, menu, and system defaults without
      mutating the caller's table.
- [ ] Make definition validation return a DwarfUI-owned copy containing the
      title, resolved pens, labels, and handler references.
- [ ] Store that validated copy at registration and replace it only through
      re-registration or an explicit update operation.
- [ ] Copy the stored validated definition into an open-session snapshot when
      the menu opens.
- [ ] Ensure caller mutation after registration affects neither the current
      registration nor an open session.
- [ ] Add documented target and anchor descriptor types in
      `context_menu/target.lua`.
- [ ] Define a documented selection-context object containing target kind,
      anchor kind, stable source identity, captured screen position, and copied
      map position where applicable.
- [ ] Store source widgets, map owners, and source roots as weak references in
      an open session; resolve them only when validating the session or
      constructing the callback context.
- [ ] Define callback-context behavior when a weak source has disappeared:
      close without invoking the handler.
- [ ] Never retain a native map object pointer when a copied coordinate or
      stable numeric identity is sufficient.
- [ ] Add a documented open-session type that owns exactly one definition
      snapshot, target descriptor, anchor descriptor, and lifecycle state.
- [ ] Add focused definition tests under `tests/context_menu/` for valid,
      invalid, empty, and partially colored definitions.
- [ ] Add focused snapshot tests proving caller mutation after registration or
      opening alters neither the next opening nor the active menu.

### Phase 1 completion gate

- [ ] Definition validation, color fallback, snapshot immutability, enum use,
      and selection context are deterministic without a live DF runtime.
- [ ] Every new class and method has LuaDoc documentation.

## Phase 2 - Implement widget and map-tile registration

- [ ] Add `context_menu/registration.lua` as the owner of public widget
      registrations and registration lifecycle.
- [ ] Preserve tooltip-like weak widget ownership so registration does not
      keep a removed widget alive.
- [ ] Make repeated registration of the same widget replace its stored
      validated definition without creating a duplicate registration,
      sequence entry, discovery loop, or input hook.
- [ ] Define registration idempotence in terms of ownership and side effects;
      do not require table identity or structural equality comparisons between
      caller definitions.
- [ ] Implement explicit widget update and unregister behavior.
- [ ] Validate widget re-registration and update inputs before mutation so a
      failed definition replacement leaves the prior registration intact.
- [ ] Resolve the widget's eligible current root dynamically instead of
      retaining a stale screen or overlay root.
- [ ] Separate attachment/root discovery from target eligibility so a hidden
      or inactive child can still identify the Lua screen whose reversible
      input wrapper must remain installed.
- [ ] Reject detached, hidden, inactive, disabled-overlay, foreign-root, or
      otherwise ineligible widget registrations at target-resolution time.
- [ ] Add `context_menu/map_target.lua` with exact copied x/y/z keys, weak
      owner semantics, and disposable registration handles.
- [ ] Define deterministic latest-registration precedence for duplicate map
      coordinates.
- [ ] Implement atomic map-handle updates for position and definition.
- [ ] Ensure a failed update leaves the prior map registration intact.
- [ ] Automatically remove map registrations when their weak owner or handle
      is no longer live.
- [ ] Start registration-gated input/root discovery when at least one live
      registration exists, regardless of its current visibility, attachment,
      overlay enablement, or presentation eligibility.
- [ ] Add `context_menu/root_discovery.lua` with one reload-safe,
      registration-gated frame callback chain for discovering attachment and
      root changes that cannot be observed from an installed input hook.
- [ ] Ensure root discovery reads only the live registered widget/map owners
      and does not scan unrelated views or world state.
- [ ] Evaluate target eligibility dynamically for each input sample and render.
- [ ] Keep enough discovery active to observe a temporarily hidden, detached,
      disabled, or unpresented registration becoming eligible again.
- [ ] Stop discovery and release hooks only when the final live registration
      disappears and no menu is open.
- [ ] Add focused registration tests for collection, detach, visibility,
      root changes, disabled overlays, duplicate map positions, failed updates,
      explicit unregister, and reload generation changes.

### Phase 2 completion gate

- [ ] Widget and map registrations have weak, reload-safe, explicitly tested
      ownership.
- [ ] Registration alone does not open a screen, consume input, attach views,
      or mutate consumer layout or presentation. It may cause the service to
      install the explicitly permitted reversible input-method wrapper.

## Phase 3 - Implement target detection and anchor capture

- [ ] Add `context_menu/input_sample.lua` to construct one immutable
      synchronous sample for each candidate opening input call.
- [ ] Read `dfhack.screen.getMousePos()` exactly once for the sample's
      screen/interface coordinate.
- [ ] When live map registrations exist, read `dfhack.gui.getMousePos()`
      exactly once for the sample's exact map coordinate.
- [ ] Use the same input sample for widget hit testing, DwarfUI blocker
      resolution, exact map lookup, target precedence, and anchor capture.
- [ ] Do not reuse the asynchronous tooltip pointer-poller state for opening;
      it can be one or more frames older than the input being consumed.
- [ ] Add `context_menu/target_detector.lua` and reuse the generic
      `dwarfui/pointer` traversal policies instead of creating a second widget
      hit tester.
- [ ] Refactor `dwarfui/pointer.lua` to publish immutable numeric
      `PointerPolicy` and `PointerResultKind` enums and return only named enum
      members.
- [ ] Migrate existing pointer consumers, widget attributes, tooltip target
      detection, package fixtures, and focused tests to named pointer enum
      members; remove string policy/result comparisons from production and
      tests.
- [ ] Update module-registry and package-contract expectations for the shared
      pointer module's new enum exports.
- [ ] Traverse overlapping UI descendants in reverse render order.
- [ ] Preserve the existing target, pass, block, and none behavior through the
      corresponding named `PointerPolicy` members.
- [ ] Across distinct eligible DwarfUI roots, choose the registered widget with
      the latest original registration sequence, matching the tooltip detector.
- [ ] If at least one registered widget target is found, ignore blockers from
      other roots. If no registered widget target is found, any blocker
      suppresses map fallback.
- [ ] Allow only a currently eligible registered widget to resolve as a widget
      context-menu target.
- [ ] Suppress map fallback when pointer traversal reports a blocking UI
      region in a traversed DwarfUI root.
- [ ] Attempt exact same-z map lookup only after widget resolution reports a
      true miss rather than a block and the synchronous sample contains a
      valid map coordinate.
- [ ] Give a widget target precedence over a map target at the same screen
      position.
- [ ] Capture the opening pointer position as a copied
      `SCREEN_POSITION` anchor for a widget target.
- [ ] Capture the registered copied world coordinate as a `MAP_TILE` anchor for
      a map target.
- [ ] Preserve stable target identity in the selection context and retain
      source widgets, map owners, and roots only through the session's defined
      weak-reference mechanism.
- [ ] Do not infer a map target from a projected marker, label, or proximity;
      resolve the exact world tile under the pointer.
- [ ] Add focused tests for within-root overlap, cross-root overlap and
      registration order, clipping, target ownership, blockers within and
      across roots, DwarfUI-over-map precedence, exact same-z matching,
      duplicate map entries, both anchor descriptor types, and pointer enum
      migration.

### Phase 3 completion gate

- [ ] One pointer sample produces exactly one eligible target, block, or miss.
- [ ] UI targets produce fixed screen anchors and map targets produce copied
      world anchors.
- [ ] A blocking region in a traversed DwarfUI root cannot accidentally expose
      a map context menu behind that DwarfUI region.

## Phase 4 - Implement the service and opening hooks

- [ ] Add `context_menu/service.lua` as the single process-wide owner of the
      active session, hook generation, and diagnostics.
- [ ] Enforce at most one active context menu process-wide.
- [ ] Expose one documented service transition for opening, closing, and
      selecting; input surfaces must not mutate session state independently.
- [ ] Add `context_menu/input_hook.lua` with reload-safe hook ownership,
      predecessor preservation, exact restoration, and foreign-wrapper safety.
- [ ] Match the tooltip render hook's wrapper contract: restore the exact
      predecessor only when DwarfUI's trampoline is still outermost; never
      overwrite a foreign outer wrapper; invalidate and diagnose an inert
      superseded trampoline that remains inside a foreign chain.
- [ ] Intercept native viewscreen input at
      `plugins.overlay.feed_viewscreen_widgets()` before delegating to its
      predecessor.
- [ ] Intercept Lua-screen input through the explicitly permitted reversible
      wrapper around the owning screen instance's effective `onInput()` method.
- [ ] Install Lua-screen hooks on attached roots that own live registrations;
      current target visibility or presentation eligibility must not be
      required to retain discovery of that root.
- [ ] Restrict opening hooks to actionable opening-target resolution. They do
      not process interaction for an already-visible context-menu screen.
- [ ] Delegate `_MOUSE_R_DOWN`-only input unchanged to the predecessor; do not
      sample, resolve, consume, or retain an opening target from that event.
- [ ] On `_MOUSE_R`, construct the synchronous input sample, resolve and
      validate the current target and anchor, consume the complete input table,
      and open from that same call.
- [ ] If `_MOUSE_R_DOWN` and `_MOUSE_R` arrive together, resolve, consume, and
      open exactly once from `_MOUSE_R` in that one input call.
- [ ] If `_MOUSE_R` resolves a miss or blocker without a map target, delegate
      the original unmodified key table to the predecessor and preserve its
      exact return values.
- [ ] If a context-menu-owned key is recognized in any input surface, consume
      its entire mixed-key table. Do not forward non-context keys from that same
      table.
- [ ] Close the session before invoking `on_select`, and prevent handler errors
      from restoring or duplicating the closed session.
- [ ] Report handler errors through one documented protected-call boundary
      owned by the service.
- [ ] Add focused service and hook tests for native and Lua seams,
      `_MOUSE_R_DOWN` transparency, actionable and coalesced `_MOUSE_R`,
      mixed-key batch consumption, misses, blockers, predecessor return
      preservation, reentrancy, reload adoption, exact outermost restoration,
      inert superseded trampolines, and foreign wrappers.

### Phase 4 completion gate

- [ ] The service is the sole authority for session state.
- [ ] Each actionable opening input table opens at most once and is consumed in
      full when it opens a menu.
- [ ] A miss is behaviorally transparent to the predecessor.
- [ ] No mouse-down latch or post-dismissal suppression state exists unless the
      Phase 0 seam evidence required an explicit checklist revision.

## Phase 5 - Implement the interactive menu screen and renderer

- [ ] Add `context_menu/screen.lua` as a short-lived `gui.ZScreen` host owned
      by one open session.
- [ ] Make `ContextMenuScreen:onInput()` the sole physical input surface for
      interaction and dismissal while the menu is visible; it delegates every
      state transition to the service.
- [ ] Configure `initial_pause=false`, `force_pause=false`,
      `pass_pause=false`, `pass_movement_keys=false`,
      `pass_mouse_clicks=false`, and `defocusable=false`. The context-menu
      screen performs filtered pause/map forwarding itself.
- [ ] Render the parent first and the context menu last so the menu is the
      topmost final paint.
- [ ] Give menu subviews first refusal on every key. If no context-menu-owned
      key was handled, forward a filtered `D_PAUSE` table for either anchor
      kind. For a map-tile session, also permit a filtered unhandled key
      recognized by `gui.dwarfmode.getMapKey()` so camera movement can occur
      without also changing menu selection.
- [ ] Preserve only the modifiers required by the forwarded pause or map action;
      never send the original mixed-key table to the parent.
- [ ] Keep list navigation, list paging, mouse wheel, selection, Escape, pause,
      right-click, and all left-click menu gestures out of movement forwarding.
- [ ] Do not forward Escape, right-click, outside left-click, entry selection,
      or any other context-menu-owned input to the parent.
- [ ] On Escape, request one service-owned close and consume the input.
- [ ] On a second actionable `_MOUSE_R`, request one service-owned close,
      consume the complete input table, and never resolve or open an underlying
      target.
- [ ] On an outside actionable `_MOUSE_L`, request one service-owned close and
      consume the complete input table.
- [ ] On an entry `_MOUSE_L`, consume the complete input table and invoke
      selection exactly once through the service's close-before-callback
      transition.
- [ ] `_MOUSE_L_DOWN` alone neither selects nor dismisses. If down and
      `_MOUSE_L` are coalesced, act exactly once from `_MOUSE_L`.
- [ ] Route keyboard selection through that same service transition.
- [ ] Add `context_menu/renderer.lua` with an optional title and a selectable,
      scrollable entry list.
- [ ] Size preferred width from the longest title or entry label plus frame
      padding, but cap final width to the available interface width.
- [ ] Deterministically right-truncate title and entry text that exceeds the
      capped content width, using an ellipsis when at least four content cells
      are available and direct clipping on narrower interfaces.
- [ ] Cap height to the available screen space and enable wheel and keyboard
      scrolling when entries do not fit.
- [ ] Fill each complete entry row with its resolved background rather than
      coloring only the label cells.
- [ ] Paint each entry with its resolved foreground and background.
- [ ] Define deterministic selected and hovered pens that preserve readable
      contrast when entries use custom colors.
- [ ] Keep the title nonselectable and omit any automatic list-count suffix.
- [ ] Support keyboard navigation and selection, left-click selection, and
      wheel scrolling.
- [ ] Clamp the final menu frame to every screen edge without changing the
      stored source anchor.
- [ ] Recompute layout after interface dimension changes.
- [ ] Dismiss the screen exactly once on selection, explicit close, source
      invalidation, reload, world unload, or owner removal.
- [ ] Add focused renderer and screen tests for an absent title, long labels,
      horizontal truncation, custom pens, selection pens, scrolling, every
      screen edge, small interfaces, resize, input ownership, filtered pause
      and movement forwarding, down-only and coalesced mouse flags, mixed-key
      batches, and all close paths.

### Phase 5 completion gate

- [ ] The menu is readable, topmost, navigable, and fully interactive with
      both default and entry-specific colors.
- [ ] Screen ownership does not pause the game or pass menu-owned input to the
      parent.
- [ ] The visible ZScreen, rather than an underlying opening hook, owns every
      open-menu interaction.
- [ ] Every dismissal path destroys one open screen and one session.

## Phase 6 - Implement screen-position and map-tile placement

- [ ] Place the preferred menu frame with its top-left cell at the source
      anchor.
- [ ] If the preferred frame overflows right or bottom, try the corresponding
      left-of-anchor or above-anchor placement before final clamping.
- [ ] Apply deterministic horizontal and vertical placement independently so
      corner behavior is testable.
- [ ] Keep a widget menu's captured screen anchor fixed while its source
      widget moves or rerenders.
- [ ] Re-clamp a widget menu after screen-size changes without changing its
      captured anchor.
- [ ] For map-tile sessions, retain a copied x/y/z world coordinate and
      re-resolve the current dwarfmode viewport before every render.
- [ ] Use the active DFHack viewport helper for text-mode world-to-screen
      projection.
- [ ] Use the active Premium viewport corner, interface dimensions, and zoom
      factor for graphics-mode projection.
- [ ] Use the projection helper's canonical interface cell for the exact map
      tile as the map menu anchor in both text and Premium modes.
- [ ] Keep the projected anchor and menu frame in one interface coordinate
      space.
- [ ] Reproject after camera panning, zoom, interface resize, and other
      viewport changes.
- [ ] Close and consume no additional input when the tile becomes offscreen,
      changes away from the displayed z-level, the world unloads, or the
      owning registration becomes invalid.
- [ ] Do not pin an offscreen map menu to the nearest screen edge; it would no
      longer appear attached to its source tile.
- [ ] Do not change viewport, cursor, game pause, or native map state merely to
      keep the menu visible.
- [ ] If a generic projection helper is extracted outside `context_menu/`,
      migrate the existing minecart projection to it in the same change so the
      helper has at least two production consumers.
- [ ] Otherwise keep the projection implementation under `context_menu/`; do
      not create a nominally generic one-consumer module or silently duplicate
      graphics-mode math.
- [ ] Add unit tests proving a screen-position anchor remains fixed.
- [ ] Add unit tests proving a map anchor moves after viewport changes and
      closes for offscreen, wrong-z, invalid-owner, and world-unload cases.
- [ ] Add text-mode and Premium projection coverage for map edges, zoom,
      interface resize, preferred placement, left/above fallback, every
      fallback combination, and final clamping.

### Phase 6 completion gate

- [ ] Widget context menus remain at their captured screen positions.
- [ ] Map context menus visibly track their world tiles every render.
- [ ] Projection invalidation closes cleanly without stale screens, map
      mutation, or input side effects.

## Phase 7 - Integrate lifecycle, reload, registry, packaging, and docs

- [ ] Make module reload invalidate stale callbacks, registrations, screens,
      and active render/input ownership from the prior generation.
- [ ] Apply the tooltip hook cleanup contract: restore only an outermost hook
      owned by this generation, never overwrite a foreign wrapper installed
      after DwarfUI, and make any superseded trampoline retained inside that
      foreign chain inert and visible in diagnostics.
- [ ] Clear the active menu and registrations on world unload and DwarfUI
      teardown.
- [ ] Add every reload-managed module to
      `dwarfui/module_registry.lua` in dependency order.
- [ ] Update module-registry tests for load and environment-clear order.
- [ ] Update package-contract tests for all
      `dwarfui/context_menu/` production modules.
- [ ] Update build/package verification so nested production and test
      directories are discovered correctly.
- [ ] Document the public registration, update, unregister, map-handle,
      definition, color-fallback, selection-context, close, and anchoring
      contracts in the project README or a dedicated guide.
- [ ] Document that the initial public API has no programmatic replacement
      operation and that a right-click while open only closes.
- [ ] Include examples for a widget screen-position menu and an exact map-tile
      menu.
- [ ] Document that selection and outside left-click both close and consume.
- [ ] Link `Docs/project.todo` to this checklist without marking the project
      item complete before every definition-of-done gate passes.

### Phase 7 completion gate

- [ ] Reload and teardown leave no stale screen, active hook ownership,
      registration, or callback. A superseded inert trampoline may remain only
      when an opaque foreign outer wrapper prevents exact restoration, matching
      the tooltip hook contract, and must be reported by diagnostics.
- [ ] Registry order, package contents, public documentation, and both anchor
      examples match the implemented API.

## Phase 8 - Add native component and interaction coverage

- [ ] Add a DwarfSpec component test for a registered DwarfUI overlay control
      whose context menu opens at the captured screen position.
- [ ] Right-click the actual rendered control through the production input
      boundary.
- [ ] Prove the opening hook samples the screen pointer synchronously and does
      not depend on the tooltip poller's previous-frame state.
- [ ] Prove `_MOUSE_R_DOWN` alone follows the audited transparent predecessor
      behavior and does not open a context menu.
- [ ] Prove the backing UI does not receive the actionable `_MOUSE_R` input
      table when it opens the menu.
- [ ] Prove an outside left-click closes and does not reach backing UI.
- [ ] Reopen and prove a second right-click closes and does not reopen or reach
      backing UI.
- [ ] Prove mixed input tables containing a context-menu-owned click and an
      otherwise forwardable key are consumed in full.
- [ ] Reopen and prove Escape closes and is consumed.
- [ ] Reopen, select an entry with a real left-click, and prove it closes,
      consumes, and calls the handler once.
- [ ] Reopen, select an entry with keyboard input, and prove it closes,
      consumes, and calls the handler once.
- [ ] Add a Lua `gui.ZScreen` component case that opens through its instance
      input seam rather than the native overlay seam.
- [ ] Add a map-tile component case against a real visible same-z map tile.
- [ ] Prove opening the map menu suppresses native map right-click behavior.
- [ ] Move the map camera while the menu remains open and prove the menu moves
      with the same source tile.
- [ ] Prove list navigation is consumed before map-movement forwarding and
      that only a filtered unhandled map key still moves the camera.
- [ ] Prove pause is a filtered forwarding exception for both anchor kinds.
- [ ] Change z-level or pan the source tile outside the visible viewport and
      prove the menu closes cleanly.
- [ ] Exercise custom title, menu colors, per-entry overrides, long lists,
      scrolling, and selection in the mounted production renderer.
- [ ] Hide and restore a registered widget without re-registering it and prove
      discovery observes its return to eligibility.
- [ ] Mutate a caller-owned definition after registration and prove neither
      the next opening nor an already-open menu changes.
- [ ] In a controlled disposable case, invalidate a weak source while its menu
      is open and prove the menu closes without invoking a handler.
- [ ] Snapshot and restore pointer, viewport, z-level, pause state, screen
      stack, overlay state, and any test-owned registrations even when an
      assertion fails.
- [ ] Require DwarfSpec `cleanup_confirmed=true` and independently inspect
      retained screens, active hooks, registrations, and callbacks.

### Phase 8 completion gate

- [ ] Production widget and map registrations open through their real input
      seams and consume opening, closing, and selection input.
- [ ] Opening hooks only open; the visible production ZScreen exclusively owns
      its interactions and dismissals.
- [ ] The screen-position anchor is fixed and the map-tile anchor tracks camera
      movement in the mounted production system.
- [ ] Cleanup is confirmed with no retained UI, active hooks, registrations, or
      changed game state, except for any inert diagnosed trampoline retained
      inside an opaque foreign wrapper under the tooltip hook contract.

## Phase 9 - Complete verification and live acceptance

- [ ] Run the focused definition, registration, root-discovery, input-sample,
      detector, pointer-enum, hook, renderer, screen, service, and projection
